  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 1152 AM MDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nUpper-air maps show an upper-level low coming off the northern \nRockies along the Canadian border this morning, with another\nshortwave close on its heels expected to move over the northern\nRockies later today. An associated cold front made its way into \nColorado early this morning that is keeping temperatures around \nfive to ten degrees cooler than Monday morning. This should keep \nafternoon high temperatures slightly cooler than yesterday, with \nonly a few places across the plains expected to approach the 90 \ndegree mark. The persistent upper-level ridge over the south \ncentral CONUS will continue to pull a plume of monsoonal moisture \ninto southeastern Colorado and keep PWATs around 160% of normal \nalong the southern portion of our forecast area. Areas expected to\nsee the best precipitation chances will generally be along and \nsouth of I-70, with potential for some strong to severe storms \nover the Palmer Divide and across Lincoln County this evening. \nWith PW values around 1-1.5\" expected, we could get a few heavy \nrainers that could lead to localized flash flooding potential, and\nexpect at least an isolated severe wind threat, mainly for Elbert\nand Lincoln Counties. The HREF guidance hones in on southern \nLincoln County likely to see the greatest rainfall accumulations, \nwith the best timing of heaviest rainfall and strong winds between\n7PM-10PM. Storms should move out of the forecast area by around \nmidnight tonight.  \n\nAnother cold front is expected across northern Colorado early \nWednesday morning that will cool temperatures by a few more \ndegrees for Wednesday afternoon. Expect some gusty northerly winds\nto move from north to south across the plains around sunrise \nfollowed by comfortable afternoon high temperatures at or slightly \nbelow normal. The cooler air isn't expected to make it into the \nhigher elevations, so our mountain valleys will likely still see \nabove-normal afternoon high temps, but still a few degrees cooler \nthan today. Mid-level flow aloft is expected to become more zonal \nas the shortwave passes to the north. This will move drier air \nfrom the west into the forecast area, limiting the monsoonal plume\nto southeastern Colorado. Mostly dry conditions are expected for \nthe forecast area outside of potential for some showers in \nsouthern Lincoln County overnight. \n\nZonal flow aloft is expected to persist into Thursday keeping\ndrier air flowing into the forecast area. 700 mb temperatures \nshould warm by a few degrees resulting in afternoon highs about \nfive to ten degrees above normal across the forecast area, but dry\nconditions are expected. Will have to watch for elevated fire\nweather conditions developing for a few hours in North Park\nThursday afternoon where single digit relative humidity values \nare expected with wind gusts approaching critical thresholds. Friday\nwill warm by a few more degrees and latest guidance suggests a \nslight increase in mid-level moisture in WNW flow may bring \nisolated afternoon showers, but most areas are expected to remain \ndry. \n\nThe northern tier looks to remain fairly active through the latter\nhalf of the period with multiple disturbances expected to move \nacross the northern Rockies. This will send a few more cold fronts\nsouth across Colorado this weekend and early next week. Guidance\nis still diverging on the strength and timing of a more pronounced\ntrough moving out of the Pacific Northwest this weekend, but for\nnow it's looking like cooler temperatures and more active weather\ncan be expected for next weekend.",
